WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.265 --> 00:00:02.410
- As a drummer,

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:04.820
you're conducting not only the orchestra

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:06.220
that is the band on stage,

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:10.690
but the orchestra that are
the people in the audience.

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:13.960
That audience relationship
was a driving factor

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.840
around why I pursued music so vehemently,

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:21.527
and why I gladly spent my time
as a broke musician (laughs).

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:25.880
But that's not sustainable.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.373
We need artists; we need artists.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.900
When I discovered Blockchain technologies,

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.480
it dawned on me that this
could empower artists

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.350
to continue to share their
creativity with the world,

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.936
which is an invaluable human resource.

00:00:41.936 --> 00:00:44.603
(music)

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.940
I played my first paying gig at age 14.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:50.750
I became so obsessed with playing drums

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.680
and taking as many gigs as I could.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.030
It fed my soul, it fed my, it's
kind of cliche to say that,

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:58.610
but it fed my creative spirit.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:00:59.970
I was behind that drum kit

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:01.690
on various stages all over the world.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:04.050
Shows where the applause from the audience

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.280
drowned out my thoughts.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.720
I was signed to an independent label,

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.120
and I'm getting millions of streams

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.260
on digital distribution platforms.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.160
I felt that I had made it,

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:16.823
and yet, I was still
struggling to make ends meet.

00:01:17.764 --> 00:01:20.120
(music cuts out)

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.430
Where the money is for artists
is in live performances,

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.280
but when it comes to event ticketing,

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:30.020
there's still a lot of
unnecessary middlemen.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:33.480
We often call them scalpers or touts.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:37.370
These people buy up
roughly 60 percent of tickets,

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.750
and then charge these exorbitant markups,

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.050
and sometimes unsuspecting fans

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.090
show up at the door of the
venue, and they can't get in.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:45.120
It's a fake ticket.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.453
So it's a major problem.

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:52.090
- Just as the internet changed
the way we communicate,

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:56.130
Blockchain is going to
change the way we transact.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.560
- [Jason] For the first
time in human history,

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:58.700
there's this technology

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.090
that shackles the brokers and scalpers.

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.850
- [Raphael] Blockchain allows
for direct transactions

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.370
between the artist and the fan.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:09.240
- And the fan knows that
the ticket is verified

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.870
and authentic, and the
money will go directly

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:13.470
into the pocket of that artist.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:16.420
- They're not adding any
value; get out of the way.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.620
The fundamental design of Blockchain

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.530
is you have a peer-to-peer network,

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:22.900
so I can do this transaction
directly with you,

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:25.960
and trust that it's
real, and you know what?

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.350
I'm excited to go see the concert.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:32.100
- The Blockchain is open,
transparent and immutable.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.630
Anyone can go to a website and
see the transactions happen,

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:39.560
whereby it's incredibly
hard to change a transaction

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:40.700
once it has occurred.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.273
Transparency creates trust,

00:02:43.273 --> 00:02:45.563
trust forms the basis of community.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.320
- The real human truth about Blockchain

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:53.550
is it's not just about changing
the way we transact money.

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:57.930
It's about leveraging the
technology’s shared record of truth

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.560
to establish trust between people,

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.460
companies or even nations,

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:05.470
and exchange what we value,

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.470
which is the whole point of a community.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.280
- The human experience is tribal,

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.970
and live experiences cannot
be digitally replicated.

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:17.140
When the music hits you,

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:20.853
it's something very real and
visceral and non-digital.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.177
It is just an incredible feeling.

00:03:24.177 --> 00:03:27.350
(upbeat music)

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:29.573
Music is inextricably a part of who I am.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:33.513
Because when it all comes crumbling down,

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:36.950
we'll still have song
and dance around a fire,

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:38.620
we're still gonna tell stories.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.290
It's part of the human experience,

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:45.346
and without it, we'd just be
a bunch of ones and zeroes.

00:03:45.346 --> 00:03:46.990
(laughs)

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:50.490
(electric guitar playing)

